\f1\b\fs22 The Hunter's Return
\f2\b0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Haloren Smythe moved through Sublime Point's corridors with the focused intensity of a man who had finally found his way back to unfinished business. The chaos of the battle outside provided perfect cover for personal vendettas, and Halo had spent months thinking about Sarah Jacobson\'97about the moment at her reception when he had been so close to completing his masterpiece, only to be thwarted by circumstances and inconvenient security arrangements.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>This time would be different. This time, the pregnant socialite would discover exactly why he had earned the sobriquet "Scarlet Shower Surgeon," and why his signature weapon bore the cheerfully ironic name "Point of Order."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The fact that he was supposed to be dead\'97officially killed at the Gravity Well restaurant\'97only added to his satisfaction. There was something deliciously poetic about a ghost returning to finish what he had started, especially when that ghost had spent considerable effort infiltrating his former handler's inner circle for exactly this opportunity.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Halo navigated the facility's layout with the precision of someone who had studied architectural plans with the devotion usually reserved for religious texts. Sarah would be in the residential section, probably in her secure quarters, possibly with minimal security given the focus on the external battle. It would be almost disappointingly easy.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Interceptors
\f2\b0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>What Halo had not anticipated was encountering Detective Superintendent Hayley Lanning and Dr. Kathy Logan in the corridor leading to the residential wing. The two women were moving with purpose toward what appeared to be their own urgent destination, and their sudden appearance transformed his carefully planned approach into an immediate tactical problem.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Haloren Smythe," Hayley said, her hand moving instinctively toward her weapon. "You're supposed to be dead."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"A greatly exaggerated report," Halo replied, producing Point of Order with practiced ease. The blade caught the corridor lighting with the kind of gleam that suggested it had been recently sharpened and was eager to justify its maintenance. "Though I'm about to make that condition considerably more widespread."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy stepped closer to Hayley, her medical training automatically cataloguing the various ways the situation could go catastrophically wrong. "You're the Scarlet Shower Surgeon. You're here for Sarah."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Eventually," Halo agreed. "But you've presented me with an interesting opportunity for an appetiser. A police detective and a doctor\'97there's a certain professional symmetry to killing representatives of law enforcement and medical care. Like eliminating the concepts of justice and healing in one efficient encounter."\

\f1\b <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Fight
\f2\b0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>What followed was a confrontation that combined Hayley's police combat training, Kathy's surprising proficiency with improvised weapons, and Halo's years of experience in the precise application of violence to unwilling participants.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley moved with the tactical precision of someone who had trained for exactly this scenario, using the corridor's geometry to limit Halo's mobility while maintaining distance from his blade. Kathy supported her with the kind of creative problem-solving that came from years of emergency medicine, deploying medical equipment as defensive tools and using her knowledge of human anatomy to target vulnerable points.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Despite their coordination, Halo proved to be exactly as dangerous as his reputation suggested. He fought with the focused intensity of someone for whom killing was not just a profession but an art form, and his blade work demonstrated the kind of precision that came from extensive practice on unwilling subjects.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"You're quite good," he commented to Hayley as he deflected her attack and forced both women back toward a wider section of corridor. "But you're fighting defensively. You're trying not to die rather than trying to kill me. That's always a losing strategy."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley circled him carefully, looking for openings. "We just need to delay you until security arrives."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Security is rather busy at the moment," Halo replied, lunging forward with a combination attack that forced both women to dodge in different directions. "And I've killed security personnel before. It's remarkably easy once you understand their training limitations."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The fight continued for several minutes, with Halo maintaining a slight but consistent advantage. His experience with killing gave him an edge that neither Hayley's training nor Kathy's determination could quite overcome. They were fighting for their lives; he was performing his life's work.\

\f1\b <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Cavalry
\f2\b0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony Chantara appeared in the corridor with the timing of someone who had either planned his arrival perfectly or possessed extraordinarily good luck. Behind him came four figures that caused Halo to pause in genuine confusion: identical men in dark suits, all bearing the distinctive facial features and paranoid intensity of Special Agent Ajent Jones.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Tony," Hayley called out, not taking her eyes off Halo. "Backup would be appreciated."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Already here," Chanta replied, gesturing to his companions. "Meet the B-Team. They've been looking forward to some field work."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The four Ajent clones moved with synchronized precision that was either extremely professional or extremely disturbing. They deployed around Halo with tactical efficiency, creating overlapping fields of fire and eliminating his escape routes with the kind of coordination that suggested they shared more than just physical appearance.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Haloren Smythe," the lead clone announced. "You are under arrest for multiple homicides, attempted murder, and general crimes against the natural order."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"I don't think so," Halo replied, but his confidence had shifted noticeably. Fighting two women, even skilled ones, was manageable. Fighting four paranoid federal agents with identical training and shared tactical awareness was a significantly different proposition.\

\f1\b <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Final Engagement
\f2\b0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The battle that followed was brief but extraordinarily violent. Halo demonstrated why he had survived years as a professional killer, using his blade with lethal efficiency and exploiting every available advantage. But the Ajent clones fought with the kind of coordinated ruthlessness that came from being purpose-built for exactly this type of confrontation.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The engagement ended when two clones managed to pin Halo's weapon arm while a third delivered what medical professionals would later describe as "catastrophic blunt force trauma to the cranial region," and what Chanta would remember as "the sound of someone hitting a watermelon with a sledgehammer."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Haloren Smythe, the Scarlet Shower Surgeon, serial killer and professional assassin, died in a corridor of Sublime Point, surrounded by people he had intended to murder and killed by government agents who were, technically speaking, not entirely real.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Is it over?" Kathy asked, checking Halo's pulse with professional thoroughness.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"For him, yes," Hayley replied, holstering her weapon. "For the rest of us..." She gestured toward the sounds of ongoing battle outside. "I think we're just getting started."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta looked down at Halo's body with something approaching sadness. "He spent so much time planning to kill people that he forgot other people might be planning to kill him."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The four Ajent clones stood in formation, awaiting further orders with the patience of men who had been designed for exactly this purpose and were now wondering what they were supposed to do with the rest of their artificially accelerated lives.\
